diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any routing or reachability instability between your site and the partner. The eBGP control-plane between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is healthy and stable, and the data-plane delivers traffic losslessly.

Evidence:
- On router1, 200.1.1.0/24 is learned via BGP from 193.10.11.2 and installed in the RIB/FIB (show ip route: “B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, eth0”). On router2, 195.11.14.0/24 is similarly learned from 193.10.11.1.
- BGP sessions are Established on both routers with steady keepalives and no neighbor resets; uptime counters ~2–3 minutes since service start and no withdrawals logged (show ip bgp neighbors; frr.log on both sides).
- End-to-end pc1→pc2 ping tests (including 1400-byte payloads and faster intervals) show 0% packet loss and low, stable RTTs.
- Interfaces are up/clean (no errors/drops via ifconfig/tc; ethtool shows link detected).

Localization: No flaps or policy changes observed on router1:eth0/eth1 or router2:eth0/eth1, nor in FRR’s BGP process on either router.

Root cause: Not found; current network state is stable and does not exhibit the reported “route disappearing” behavior.

Corrective fix: None required at this time. If the issue reappears, capture timestamps and we can enable bgp log-neighbor-changes (already active) and poll “show ip route”/“show ip bgp” to correlate. Also consider continuous ping/iperf from pc1 to 200.1.1.2 while tailing /var/log/frr/frr.log for any future churn. === END ===